<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">This Land Is Your Land</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">written 23 February <a href="/years/1940">1940</a>; first recorded April <a href="/years/1944">1944</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The American folk singer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/woody-guthrie">Woody Guthrie</a> wrote &quot;This Land Is Your Land&quot; on 23 February 1940, in his room at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a> House hotel on 43rd Street in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>. He wrote it in critical response to Irving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>'s &quot;God Bless America&quot;: tired of hearing Kate Smith sing that song on the radio in the late 1930s, he first called his own, sarcastically, &quot;God Blessed America for Me&quot;.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The American folk singer Woody Guthrie wrote &quot;<b>This Land Is Your Land</b>&quot; on 23 February 1940, in his room at the Hanover House hotel on 43rd Street in New York.</blockquote><a href="/songs/this-land-is-your-land">This Land Is Your Land</a></li></ul></section>
